// THREE ties in one column, with the FRONT tie low in the staff — the only regime in which
// the column's joint search moves a tie that no greedy order could have moved.
//
// LilyPond scores a whole Ties_configuration and varies its ties together
// (lily/tie-formatting-problem.cc:915-1001 generate_configuration / find_best_variation). The
// last pass, generate_extremal_tie_variations (:1086-1118), pushes the column's OUTER ties one
// half-space further out — the FRONT one for reasons that live in the rest of the column. A
// solve that fixes the front before the ties above it exist cannot take that step at all.
//
// ⚠️ WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS. Session 82 gave TieFormattingProblem the whole column and closed
// audit/lp-geometry tie.y.triad.lower (+0.250000 -> EXACT): the front tie moved from the base
// -7 to the extremal -8. NOT ONE SNAPSHOT MOVED. The corpus does hold three-tie chords —
// grammar-tour and feature-tour print <g b d>, tab-chord-tie prints <c' e' g'> — but in all of
// them the front head sits near the middle line, where the BASE configuration wins outright.
//
// ⚠️ AND THE OBVIOUS ACCOUNT OF THAT — "the extremal pass has no observer" — IS FALSE. MEASURED
// by deleting the pass and running the snapshots: test/tie-seconds fails too. What that book
// watches is the BACK tie, the one a bottom-up greedy solve reached last and could move anyway.
// Delete only the d = -1 half, the FRONT one, and before this fixture existed NOT ONE snapshot
// noticed. So the hole was never the branch; it was the half of the branch that is the whole
// reason the column went to one problem. This book is that half's first observer.
//
//   bar 1  <c e g>  front head at staff position -6, four half-spaces below the middle line.
//          This is the LilyPond twin of probe TW3 (audit/lp-geometry/probes/tie-direction.ly),
//          the book where the front takes -8 instead of -7.
//   bar 2  <g b d'> front head at -2, the corpus regime: the base wins and the extremal pass
//          changes nothing. It is the CONTROL — without it a snapshot that moved would not say
//          WHICH regime moved, and a front tie shifted a quarter space by some unrelated
//          constant would look exactly like the branch under test.
//
// ⚠️ THE WIDTHS CANNOT SEE THIS. All six triad WIDTH points opened EXACT while the height was
// off: LilyPond gives the same c the same 3.875445 span at -7 and at -8. The tie's attachment
// HEIGHT is the only place the chosen position shows, which is why this fixture has to be a
// drawing and not a width.
//
// ✔ VERIFIED TO GUARD, not assumed to. Two runs, because the second is the one that says what
// this book adds over the corpus that was already there:
//   whole extremal pass removed        tie-seconds AND this book fail   (200 pass / 2 fail)
//   only the FRONT half (d = -1)       THIS BOOK ALONE fails            (201 pass / 1 fail)
// A fixture that does not move when the thing it names moves is the very hole it claims to
// fill, so the check is part of adding it.

octave absolute

title "Tied Triads"
composer "Lily#"

time 4/4
key c major

part melody { clef treble }

section Main {
  melody {
    <c e g>2 ~ <c e g>2 |
    <g b d'>2 ~ <g b d'>2 |
  }
}

form main { Main }

score main "tie-triad-extremal" {
  staff melody
}
